% ============================================================================
% triangle-alt-name — for the triangle side-class where the SAME source
% names an alternate word, the alternate name (adj-facts-stdlib, GEOMETRY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `triangle-types.adj`
% (`triangle_sides(triangle, condition)`, ONE defining side-condition per
% triangle side-class: equilateral, isosceles, scalene). That table's
% `condition` column has room for only the defining CONDITION; the SAME
% already-quoted MathWorld equilateral sentence also states an ALTERNATE
% NAME for it that never made it into a row:
%
%   equilateral → regular
%     "An equilateral triangle is a triangle with all three sides of equal
%      length a, corresponding to what could also be known as a 'regular'
%      triangle."
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? triangle_alt_name(equilateral, $AltName)      % regular
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like
% `triangle-types.adj`'s own `triangle_sides`): each `row` lowers to a
% ground relation `triangle_alt_name(triangle, alt_name)` carrying the
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the alternate name WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls, and abstains on isosceles and scalene, whose own
% already-cited spans in `triangle-types.adj` state no alternate name to
% decode here.
%
% Only ONE of `triangle-types.adj`'s three side-classes has a genuinely
% distinct alternate name in the ALREADY-cited spans; this table is
% deliberately narrow rather than inventing a name the source does not
% state. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind an alternate name and recall
% which side-class it names:
%
%     ? triangle_alt_name($Triangle, regular)   % equilateral
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME equilateral sentence
% already quoted inside `triangle-types.adj`'s own provenance block, from
% the SAME MathWorld "Equilateral Triangle" page that table already cites
% — no new WebFetch, only a different schema decoding a clause of an
% already-verified quote. An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope
% (per `triangle-types.adj`'s own convention), so `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold that full sentence — the single span that fixes this
% table's only row. `trust authoritative` — the same tier
% `triangle-types.adj` already carries (Wolfram MathWorld, a widely-cited
% mathematics reference).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table triangle_alt_name {
    columns triangle, alt_name

    row (equilateral, regular)

    source "An equilateral triangle is a triangle with all three sides of equal length a, corresponding to what could also be known as a 'regular' triangle."
    locator "https://mathworld.wolfram.com/EquilateralTriangle.html"
    trust authoritative
}
